## 4. Security Architecture

### 4.1 Zero Trust Architecture

> Every request is treated as untrusted regardless of network origin.

```
Request Flow:
Client → WAF → CDN → API Gateway (mTLS) → Service Mesh (Istio) → Service
                          │
                    ┌─────▼──────┐
                    │ Verify:    │
                    │ JWT Token  │
                    │ Device ID  │
                    │ IP Reputation│
                    │ Rate Limit │
                    └────────────┘
```

### 4.2 Authentication Security Layers

| Layer | Technology | Purpose |
|-------|-----------|---------|
| Phone OTP | Firebase Auth | Primary identity verification |
| JWT Access Token | RS256 signed (2048-bit RSA) | Short-lived API access (15 min) |
| Refresh Token | Opaque, rotated | Long-lived session (30 days) |
| Device Binding | Device fingerprint hash | Prevent token theft |
| mTLS | Service mesh | Internal service communication |

### 4.3 Security Controls

| Control | Implementation |
|---------|---------------|
| Rate Limiting | Redis sliding window per IP, per user, per endpoint |
| DDoS Protection | WAF + Cloudflare / AWS Shield |
| Bot Detection | Behavioral analysis + CAPTCHA fallback |
| Anti-Spam | Pattern matching + ML classification |
| CSRF Protection | SameSite=Strict cookies + custom header validation |
| XSS Protection | Content-Security-Policy headers + DOMPurify |
| SQL Injection | Parameterized queries only (Sequelize ORM) |
| File Upload Security | Magic byte validation + MIME type check + virus scan |
| Malware Scanning | ClamAV integration in media pipeline |
| Secrets Management | HashiCorp Vault / AWS Secrets Manager |

### 4.4 Multi-Device Session Security

```
Device Registration Flow:
1. User authenticates via Firebase OTP
2. Device generates local key pair (X25519)
3. Public key sent to Device Service + Encryption Service
4. Server issues device-bound JWT
5. Device stored in trusted device list
6. Session created in Redis (TTL: 30 days)

Device Revocation Flow:
1. User requests revocation from any trusted device
2. Session Service invalidates Redis session
3. Device Service marks device as revoked
4. Encryption Service revokes device keys
5. Push notification to revoked device
```

